Here's a script that does this. I've split it into a library module and a small command-line program, because the checking is the part you'll want to reuse, and once it's in a module it can be tested on its own.

palindrome.pm:

```perl
package palindrome;

use strict;
use warnings;
use Exporter 'import';

our @EXPORT_OK = qw(is_palindrome normalise);

# Turn "A man, a plan..." into "amanaplan". Lowercase, no spaces, no junk.
sub normalise {
    my ($string) = @_;
    $string //= '';          # an undefined argument becomes ""
    $string =~ s/^\s+|\s+$//g;   # strip leading and trailing whitespace
    $string =~ s/[^[:alpha:]]//g;  # keep only letters, whatever the encoding
    return lc($string);
}

# Return true if the string reads the same backwards.
# Empty strings and strings with no letters at all (a string of punctuation)
# come out as palindromes, because every character matches itself.
# That's the usual convention, and it's worth knowing about.
sub is_palindrome {
    my ($string) = @_;
    my $clean = normalise($string);
    my $reversed = reverse $clean;
    return $clean eq $reversed;
}

1;
```

The character class says what it wants kept, letters, and what it drops is everything else: spaces, and punctuation marks, and digits. That's the same for every input, and the reason is one sentence. If it keeps something for one string and drops it for the next, the comparison is comparing two different things.

Note that it strips leading and trailing whitespace before it does anything else. "racecar " and "  racecar" both become "racecar". If you'd rather treat them as different inputs, remove that line, and I'd call it a feature either way.

One thing about `[:alpha:]`. Whether a character counts as a letter is a property of the Unicode data that comes with Perl, and it changes when Perl does. So the result of `is_palindrome("café")` is as stable as the Perl version allows, and it may drift between releases. For plain English text there's no problem. For a project that will compare results across Perl versions, take the check out of the loop and compare a hashed form instead.

The program:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/env perl
# palindromes.pl
# Check whether a string is a palindrome.
# Usage:  perl palindromes.pl "string to check"
# Or run without arguments to type strings in one at a time.

use strict;
use warnings;

use FindBin;
use lib "$FindBin::Bin";   # finds palindrome.pm in the same directory
use palindrome qw(is_palindrome normalise);

my $prompt = "\nType a string (or an empty line to quit): ";

sub say_no {
    my ($msg) = @_;
    print "Not a palindrome.\n";
    print "$msg\n" if $msg;
}

sub say_yes {
    my ($msg) = @_;
    print "That's a palindrome.\n";
    print "$msg\n" if $msg;
}

if (@ARGV) {
    # One argument on the command line, or one per argument
    for my $arg (@ARGV) {
        check($arg);
    }
} else {
    # Interactive mode
    print "Palindrome checker. Type a string and press Enter.\n";
    print "An empty line ends the session.\n";
    while (1) {
        print $prompt;
        my $line = <STDIN>;
        last unless defined $line;
        chomp $line;
        check($line);
    }
}

sub check {
    my ($input) = @_;
    my $clean = normalise($input);

    if (!length $clean) {
        say_no("The string was empty or contained no letters.");
        return;
    }

    if (is_palindrome($input)) {
        say_yes("Read backwards, it's: " . reverse($clean));
    } else {
        say_no("Read backwards, it's: " . reverse($clean));
    }
}
```

Run it as `perl palindromes.pl "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"`. Run it with no arguments to type things in one at a time.

The validation is there because `normalise` turns an empty string into a palindrome, and an empty string tells you nothing about the input. The script says so when it happens.

A few things are up to you. I'd leave the module alone. If someone finds a bug in `normalise`, they'll want the same bug in both places, and one test covers both. The interactive mode is a guess. Someone who wants to pipe files into the script will find it annoying, and they can do that anyway, since any non-empty argument goes straight through without prompting. If it becomes the common case, it's one line to change. If you'd rather it didn't print the reversed string, that's your script, and the change is about three lines.

Here is the implementation. I have written it as a class with the matching logic separated from the user interaction, so the core can be tested on its own. The input comes from the console, and I have kept the prompting plain. A program that reads a pattern from a user and then uses it as a regular expression is doing something I would want checked, so the first thing I looked at is what happens to the pattern. It goes into a replacement, or into a search, and the string is only data, never code. Here it is never code, because `String.matches` takes a pattern as an argument. The one place a pattern becomes code is `Pattern.compile`, and that is where the error handling belongs.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;

public class LineMatchCounter {

    /**
     * Counts the lines in a text that match a given regex.
     * Empty lines are counted if they match. A text with no lines returns 0.
     */
    public static long countMatchingLines(String text, String regex) {
        if (text == null) {
            return 0;
        }
        if (regex == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("The regex pattern cannot be null.");
        }

        try {
            Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile(regex);
            return pattern.splitAsStream(text)
                    .filter(line -> line.matches(regex))
                    .count();
        } catch (java.util.regex.PatternSyntaxException e) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "The pattern is not a valid regular expression. " + e.getMessage(), e);
        }
    }

    /**
     * Runs the program from a console. Reads a text and a pattern from the user,
     * then prints the count and the matching lines, one per line.
     * The program reads until EOF on the text (Ctrl+D or Ctrl+Z).
     */
    public static void run() {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter the text (one or more lines, end with EOF): ");
        String text = scanner.nextLine();
        if (text.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("No text was entered, so there is nothing to count.");
            return;
        }

        System.out.print("Enter the regular expression: ");
        String regex = scanner.nextLine();

        try {
            long count = countMatchingLines(text, regex);
            System.out.println("\n--- Results ---");
            System.out.println("Lines matching: " + count);

            // The same logic, now used for output, so the user sees exactly what was counted.
            Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile(regex);
            System.out.println("Matching lines:");
            pattern.splitAsStream(text).forEach(line -> {
                if (line.matches(regex)) {
                    System.out.println("  > " + line);
                }
            });

        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        scanner.close();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        run();
    }
}
```

The split keeps empty lines, so a blank line that matches the pattern counts, and I have said so in the comment, because whether it should is a fair thing to argue about. Most people who write this program would want blank lines counted. Someone writing a log parser might not, and the fix is one argument, so it is a small thing to argue about. The count and the list use the same logic. If they ever disagree, the count is wrong and the list will show you why.

The error handling covers the cases you asked for. An invalid pattern throws an exception, and the message names the problem and gives the reason. A missing pattern is treated the same way. An empty text is allowed, and it produces a count of zero. The program does not stop there and tell the user it gave up. It says plainly that there is nothing to count, and zero is the honest answer either way. I have chosen to let a bad pattern stop the program. Reading the text again would leave the user staring at a count of zero with no idea why.

